## Layered architecture (API)

- **presentation**: HTTP only (Express routes/controllers). No DB/Docker/Caddy calls here.
- **application**: orchestrates use-cases; depends on ports/interfaces, not concrete infra.
- **domain**: pure business rules + types (e.g. allowed status transitions). No IO.
- **infrastructure**: concrete adapters used by the **backend API** (Postgres repos, **Docker client**, Railpack runner, Caddy client).

## What “Docker client” means (backend only)

The backend needs to:
- build images (via Railpack CLI),
- run containers (via Docker),
- inspect/stop containers.

So the API has an infrastructure adapter that talks to the **Docker Engine** (e.g. via the Docker socket). 

## Dependency direction

`presentation → application → domain`

Infrastructure is injected into application via interfaces (ports).

## Rule of thumb

If it touches the network/filesystem/processes, it belongs in **infrastructure**.
